    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d122">

                <pb facs="0146" n="132" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg132"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/7–2144: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-07-21T19:00:00+07:00">July 21, 1944—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 22—10:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1264. Hollington Tong<anchor n="41" xml:id="d122-f1"/> spoke casually to the
                    Counselor at a dinner party in regard to subject of Department’s 966, July 17.
                    He said he had exchanged telegrams with Lattimore<anchor n="42" xml:id="d122-f2"
                    /> (through OWI Department doubtless having seen the messages) and he was
                    wondering whether it would not be best just to drop the matter. Atcheson replied
                    in affirmative, pointing out that it was much better to let it drop than to give
                    the article emphasis and additional publicity.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d122-f1" n="41" xml:id="d122-f1-content">
                <p>Chinese Vice Minister of Information.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d122-f2" n="42" xml:id="d122-f2-content">
